{:edb/db-before-t 12438 :edb/db-after-t 12439 :edb/tx-data [[13194139545751 50 #inst "2026-11-03T19:26:29.444Z" 13194139545751 17592186576411 true] [17592186281552 1074 "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-2y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $x=y=0$ which yields the critical point $(0,0).$ Notice that this point is not in the region's interior. However, it is in the boundary of the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1.$\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=4{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨8x,2(y-1)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x &=8\\lambda x \\\\ -2y &=2\\lambda (y-1)\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-4\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ y &=-\\lambda (y-1)\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(0)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,y=1\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,0)$ and $(0,2).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4}$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies \n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{4}(y-1)\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\n \nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{5}-1)}^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm \\frac{3}{10},\\frac{1}{5}).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(0,2),\\,(\\pm \\frac{3}{10},\\frac{1}{5})\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(\\pm \\frac{3}{10},\\frac{1}{5})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $-4$ | $\\frac{1}{20}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(0,2),$ and its minimum value is $-4.$" 13194139545751 17592186576411 false] [17592186281552 1074 "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-2y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $x=y=0$ which yields the critical point $(0,0).$ Notice that this point is not in the region's interior. However, it is in the boundary of the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1.$\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=4{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨8x,2(y-1)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x &=8\\lambda x \\\\ -2y &=2\\lambda (y-1)\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-4\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ y &=-\\lambda (y-1)\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(0)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,y=1\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,0)$ and $(0,2).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4}$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies \n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{4}(y-1)\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\n \nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}-1\\right)}^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(\\pm \\frac{3}{10},\\frac{1}{5}\\right).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(0,2),\\,\\left(\\pm \\frac{3}{10},\\frac{1}{5}\\right)\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $\\left(\\pm \\frac{3}{10},\\frac{1}{5}\\right)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $-4$ | $\\frac{1}{20}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(0,2),$ and its minimum value is $-4.$" 13194139545751 17592186576411 true] [17592186281554 1074 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+t,\\,y={t}^{3}+{t}^{2},\\,z=2{t}^{2}+2t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2{t}^{2}+2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+({t}^{2}+t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(3{t}^{2}+2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(4t+2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2{t}^{2}+2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+({t}^{2}+t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot ((2t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(3{t}^{2}+2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(4t+2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})(2t+1)+(2{t}^{2}+2t)(3{t}^{2}+2t)+({t}^{2}+t)(4t+2) \\\\  & =2t+11{t}^{2}+17{t}^{3}+8{t}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(2t+11{t}^{2}+17{t}^{3}+8{t}^{4})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[{t}^{2}+\\frac{11}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{17}{4}{t}^{4}+\\frac{8}{5}{t}^{5}]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-(1-\\frac{11}{3}+\\frac{17}{4}-\\frac{8}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{60}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545751 17592186576411 false] [17592186281554 1074 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contain