rac{3}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}{y}^{4}\\right]}_{y=0}^{y=\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{3}{8}{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{3}\\right]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =96+16 \\\\  & =112.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545751 17592186576411 true] [17592186281623 1074 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFrom the description, the mass density function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=k|x|=kx,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find an expression for $k$ in terms of $M$ by integrating the density function over the plate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}kx\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =k({\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =k{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot [y]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =k(\\frac{1}{2}-0)(2-0) \\\\  & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=M$ and hence $\\lambda (x,y)=kx=Mx.$\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =M{[\\frac{1}{4}y+\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{3