hat $v(1)=2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n2={1}^{2}+\\frac{1}{1}+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{2}+\\frac{1}{t}.$\nIn particular, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1,$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(1)=2\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=2,$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(2)={2}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{9}{2}\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =81-16 \\\\  & =65\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545751 17592186576411 false] [17592186284402 1074 "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe acceleration of the particle is given by $a(t)=2t-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},$ where $t$ is time in seconds.\nWe find the velocity $v(t)$ of the particle by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\left(2t-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+\\frac{1}{t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(1)=2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n2={1}^{2}+\\frac{1}{1}+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{2}+\\frac{1}{t}.$\nIn particular, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1,$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(1)=2\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=2,$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(2)={2}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{9}{2}\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, 