ext{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe consider the region $R$ as a type III region enclosed between the back surface $y={u}_{1}(x,z)=0$ and the front surface $y={u}_{2}(x,z)=2.$\n\n![](images/63/63cffdee00edd80c4a0abdc30e8e45b1af29078b8bc4902bf3f60b7b4a104172.png)\n\nSo, the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type II *plane* region in the $xz$-plane with the right curve\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡z.\n$$\n\n![](images/dc/dc72aeb4b554d88f00083dc6bf6f8ca4244e1b7683c8dec10ba0491c678a5603.png)\n\nHence, $D={(x,z)\\,:\\,1\\le z\\le e,\\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡z}.$\n\nTherefore, we write the triple integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{\\pi x}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi y}{4}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\pi x}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi y}{4}\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}x[{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi y}{4}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}x{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi y}{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545751 17592186576411 false] [17592186284415 1074 "For a type III region $R$ with projection $D$ onto the $xz$-plane, the triple integral of a continuous function $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe consider the region $R$ as a type III region enclosed between the back surface $y={u}_{1}(x,z)=0$ and the front surface $y={u}_{2}(x,z)=2.$\n\n![](images/63/63cffdee00edd80c4a0abdc30e8e45b1af29078b8bc4902bf3f60b7b4a104172.png)\n\nSo, the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type II *plane* region in the $xz$-plane with the right curve\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡z.\n$$\n\n![](images/dc/dc72aeb4b554d88f00083dc6bf6f8ca4244e1b7683c8dec10ba0491c678a5603.png)\n\nHence, $D={(x,z)\\,:\\,1\\le z\\le e,\\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡z}.$\n\nTherefore, we write the triple integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{\\pi x}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi y}{4}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∬}_{D}\\left[{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\pi x}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi y}{4}\\text{d}y\\right]\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}x\\left[{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi y}{4}\\,\\text{d}y\\right]\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}x{\\left[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi y}{4}\\right]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545751 17592186576411 true] [17592186284416 1074 "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ about an axis of rotation $l$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function of the lamina, and $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the line $l$ to the point $(x,y)$ on $D.$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 2},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/f5/f5a73161415455032a7fd2031dba8f1c56a7ccb40bf6dc3e4fa405a69aee74d8.png)\n\nAlso, the distance between a point and the line $y=2$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=|y-2|.\n$$\n\nNext, the mass density can be written as $\\lambda (x,y)=k|x|=kx,$ where $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find the value of $k$ by integrating the mass density function over the plate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{4}kx\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{2}x[y]_{{x}^{2}}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{2}x(4-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{2}(4x-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{[2{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =k(8-4) \\\\  & =4k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=kx=\\frac{M}{4}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the line $y=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{l} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{4}\\frac{M}{4}x\\cdot |y-2{|}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{4}\\frac{M}{4}x\\cdot (y-2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x{[\\frac{(y-2)^{3}}{3}]}_{{x}^{2}}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x(\\frac{8}{3}-\\frac{({x}^{2}-2)^{3}}{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{12}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(8x-x({x}^{2}-2)^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{12}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(-{x}^{7}+6{x}^{5}-12{x}^{3}+16)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{12}{[-\\frac{{x}^{8}}{8}+{x}^{6}-3{x}^{4}+16x]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{12}(-32+64-48+32) \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545751 17592186576411 false] [17592186284416 1074 "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ about an axis of rotation $l$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function of the lamina, and $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the line $l$ to the point $(x,y)$ on $D.$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 2},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/f5/f5a73161415455032a7fd2031dba8f1c56a7ccb40bf6dc3e4fa405a69aee74d8.png)\n\nAlso, the distance between a point and the line $y=2$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=|y-2|.\n$$\n\nNext, the mass density can be written as $\\lambda (x,y)=k|x|=kx,$ where $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find the value of $k$ by integrating the mass density function over the plate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{4}kx\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{2}x[y]_{{x}^{2}}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{2}x(4-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{2}(4x-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\left[2{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}\\right]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =k(8-4) \\\\  & =4k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=kx=\\frac{M}{4}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the line $y=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{l} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{4}\\frac{M}{4}x\\cdot |y-2{|}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{4}\\frac{M}{4}x\\cdot (y-2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x{\\left[\\frac{(y-2)^{3}}{3}\\right]}_{{x}^{2}}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\left(\\frac{8}{3}-\\frac{({x}^{2}-2)^{3}}{3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{12}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(8x-x({x}^{2}-2)^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{12}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(-{x}^{7}+6{x}^{5}-12{x}^{3}+16)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{12}{\\left[-\\frac{{x}^{8}}{8}+{x}^{6}-3{x}^{4}+16x\\right]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{12}(-32+64-48+32) \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545751 17592186576411 true] [17592186284437 1074 "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\\,,\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}y & ={y}^{2}x \\\\ y\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}y-{y}^{2}x & =0 \\\\ y(\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}-yx) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}-yx & =0 \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}+\\,\\underbrace{(-x)}_{P(x)}y & =\\underbrace{-{x}^{2}}_{Q(x)} \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}-xy & =-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=-x$ and $Q(x)=-{x}^{2}.$" 13194139545751 17592186576411 false] [17592186284437 1074 "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\\,,\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}y & ={y}^{2}x \\\\ y\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}y-{y}^{2}x & =0 \\\\ y\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}-yx\\right) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}-yx & =0 \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}+\\,\\underbrace{(-x)}_{P(x)}y & =\\underbrace{-{x}^{2}}_{Q(x)} \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}-xy & =-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=-x$ and $Q(x)=-{x}^{2}.$" 13194139545751 17592186576411 true] [17592186284442 1071 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139545751 17592186576411 false] [17592186284442 1071 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $\\left(-\\frac{1}{2},1\\right)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139545751 17592186576411 true] [17592186284442 1074 "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(-\\frac{1}{2},1),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1/2,1)} & ={\\left(\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}\\right)|}_{(-1/2,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{2xy}{(1+{x}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2xy}{(1+{x}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{y(1-2x)}{(1+{x}^{2})^{2}} \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(-1/2,1)} & ={\\left(\\frac{y(1-2x)}{(1+{x}^{2})^{2}}\\right)|}_{(-1/2,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1(1-2\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right))}{{(1+{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$(-\\frac{1}{2},1)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$” is false. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1/2,1)}=\\frac{4}{5}\\ne 0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(-\\frac{1}{2},1)$ is not a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement “$(-\\frac{1}{2},1)$ is a local maximum point of the function $f$” is false. There is no critical point at $(-\\frac{1}{2},1).$ Therefore we cannot have a local minimum at that point.\n\n- Statement “$(-\\frac{1}{2},1)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$” is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(-\\frac{1}{2},1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\"" 13194139545751 17592186576411 false] [17592186284442 1074 "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $\\left(-\\frac{1}{2},1\\right),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1/2,1)} & ={\\left(\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}\\right)|}_{(-1/2,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{\\l