751 17592186576411 true] [17592186290410 1074 "We need to identify whether $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is a critical point. If it is, we then need to classify it using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x={\\cos }^{2}⁡x-{\\sin }^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =0 \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi , \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.$ We're interested in the critical point $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at this critical point. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})=-2<0.\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is negative at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ so this critical point is a relative *maximum*. \n\nTherefore, comparing our results with the given statements, we conclude that:\n\n- $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is a critical point of $f(x)\\,✓$\n\n- ${f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)<0\\,✓$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative *maximum* at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is a critical point of $f(x)$”; ${f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)<0$ only." 13194139545751 17592186576411 false] [17592186290410 1074 "We need to identify whether $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is a critical point. If it is, we then need to classify it using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get