(x)=2{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}+20x-12$ has a root in the interval $\\left(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{5}{2}\\right).$ In **ascending order**, the **distinct** real roots of $f(x)$ are\n\n$x=$\n {{field-1}}$,\\,x=$\n {{field-2}}" 13194139545751 17592186576411 true] [17592186292903 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-12$ and the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-12}{\\text{factor of }2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm \\frac{3}{2},\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 6,\\,\\pm 12\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{5}{2})$ are $\\frac{3}{2}$ and $2.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x-\\frac{3}{2})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $2$ | $-11$ | $20$ | $-12$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $3$ | $-12$ | $12$ |\n|  | $2$ | $-8$ | $8$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x-\\frac{3}{2})$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-\\frac{3}{2})(2{x}^{2}-8x+8).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-\\frac{3}{2})(2{x}^{2}-8x+8) \\\\  & =2(x-\\frac{3}{2})({x}^{2}-4x+4) \\\\  & =(2x-3){(x-2)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find that, in ascending order, the roots of the polynomial are $x=\\frac{3}{2}$  and $x=2.$" 13194139545751 17592186576411 false] [17592186292903 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational