-3\\right)$ and $B.$ Find the coordinates of $B.$\n\nThe coordinates of $B$ are $($\n{{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139545751 17592186576411 true] [17592186292930 1074 "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=8x-1 \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{2x}-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x-1 & =\\frac{1}{2x}-1 \\\\ 8x & =\\frac{1}{2x} \\\\ 16{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{4}.$ To find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the second equation.\n\n- Substituting $x=-\\frac{1}{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =8x-1 \\\\  & =8\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)-1 \\\\  & =-2-1 \\\\  & =-3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $(-\\frac{1}{4},-3).$\n\n- Substituting $x=\\frac{1}{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =8x-1 \\\\  & =8\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)-1 \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $(\\frac{1}{4},1).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $A(-\\frac{1}{4},-3)$ and $B(\\frac{1}{4},1).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/bb/bb69eb0150879f215fe453e596ae18273ec149f1d1486c8d7dfe7b2c1e3e5e58.png)" 13194139545751 17592186576411 false] [17592186292930 1074 "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=8x-1 \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{2x}-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x-1 & =\\frac{1}{2x}-1 \\\\ 8x & =\\frac{1}{2x} \\\\ 16{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{4}.$ To find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the second equation.\n\n- Substituting $x=-\\frac{1}{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =8x-1 \\\\  & =8\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)-1 \\\\  & =-2-1 \\\\  & =-3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $\\left(-\\frac{1}{4},-3\\right).$\n\n- Substituting $x=\\frac{1}{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =8x-1 \\\\  & =8\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)-1 \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $\\left(\\frac{1}{4},1\\right).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $A\\left(-\\frac{1}{4},-3\\right)$ and $B\\left(\\frac{1}{4},1\\right).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/bb/bb69eb0150879f215fe453e596ae18273ec149f1d1486c8d7dfe7b2c1e3e5e58.png)" 13194139545751 17592186576411 true] [17592186292931 1074 "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=\\frac{2}{x}-5 \\\\ y &=8x+1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x}-5 & =8x+1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{x} & =8x+6 \\\\ 2 & =8{x}^{2}+6x \\\\ 8{x}^{2}+6x-2 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+3x-1 & =0 \\\\ (4x-1)(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{4},-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ and $x=-1.$ To find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the second equation.\n\n- Substituting $x=\\frac{1}{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =8x+1 \\\\  & =8\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)+1 \\\\  & =2+1 \\\\  & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $(\\frac{1}{4},3).$\n\n- Substituting $x=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =8x+1 \\\\  & =8(-1)+1 \\\\  & =-8+1 \\\\  & =-7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $(-1,-7).$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the points of intersection are $A(-1,-7)$ and $B(\