,$ we have $|-12x+5|=-12x+5.$ Consequently, for $x\\to -\\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{|-12x+5|}{4x+3}=\\frac{-12x+5}{4x+3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{1}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-12x+5}{4x+3} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{-12x}{x}+\\frac{5}{x}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{4x}{x}+\\frac{3}{x}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{\\left(-12+\\frac{5}{x}\\right)}{\\left(4+\\frac{3}{x}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-12+0}{4+0} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545751 17592186576411 true] [17592186314801 1074 "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=(x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2)^{′}=-\\frac{1}{x}+1\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{n}-2}{(-\\frac{1}{{x}_{n}}+1)} \\\\  & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}({x}_{n}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{n}-2)}{{x}_{n}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}({x}_{n}-1)-{x}_{n}({x}_{n}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{n}-2)}{{x}_{n}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{n}+1)}{{x}_{n}-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0.1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{{x}_{0}(\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{0}+1)}{{x}_{0}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.1\\,(\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.1)+1)}{0.1-1} \\\\  & =0.144…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{{x}_{1}(\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{1}+1)}{{x}_{1}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.144…(\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.144…)+1)}{0.144⋯-1} \\\\  & =0.157…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{{x}_{2}(\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{2}+1)}{{x}_{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\fr